+# Product Requirements Document: Pulseboard
+
+**Project:** Pulseboard  
+**Version:** 1.0.0  
+**Date:** 2026-08-10  
+**Status:** Planning  
+**Scale:** Level 2
+
+## Information sources
+
+This plan is derived only from `project.md`, `product-contract.md`, `evidence-contract.md`, `context.md`, and `architecture.md`. There are no research reports, brownfield artifacts, design-system artifacts, or constitution in this workspace. Assumptions below are explicitly reversible.
+
+## Executive summary
+
+Pulseboard helps meeting facilitators turn a transcript into reviewed action items and send one reminder only after the facilitator confirms the action text, owner, and due date. The four-week pilot targets five teams, seeking an 80% proposal-review rate and 30% fewer unowned overdue actions. It excludes recording meetings, replacing calendars, and autonomous outbound messaging.
+
+## Product vision and strategy
+
+### Vision
+
+Make post-meeting follow-through calm and accountable: facilitators begin with a useful proposal, retain the final decision, and can see whether the agreed reminder was delivered.
+
+### Strategic objectives
+
+1. Convert uploaded transcripts into reviewable action proposals (PRM-001).
+2. Make explicit confirmation the non-bypassable boundary before scheduling or delivery (PRM-002).
+3. Prove trustworthy delivery status and workspace isolation during the pilot (PRM-003, PRM-004).
+
+### Success definition
+
+- Five pilot teams use the reviewed workflow in four weeks.
+- At least 80% of proposed actions are reviewed by a facilitator.
+- Pilot teams report 30% fewer unowned overdue actions than their starting baseline.
+- Runtime evidence exercises upload → confirmation → delivery and a real-role cross-workspace negative probe.
+
+## Users and primary journey
+
+### Facilitator
+
+The facilitator leaves a meeting with a transcript and needs to turn it into accountable follow-through without accidentally messaging people from an unreviewed AI suggestion.
+
+1. Upload a meeting transcript to the correct workspace.
+2. Review generated proposals, correct action text, owner, or due date as needed.
+3. Explicitly confirm each action when it is ready.
+4. See whether its one reminder was delivered or failed.
+
+### Action owner
+
+The owner receives the one reminder associated with a facilitator-confirmed action. Owner-facing calendar replacement, repeated campaigns, and autonomous reminders are out of scope.
+
+## Functional requirements
+
+### FR-001: Transcript intake and proposal extraction — P0
+
+- A facilitator SHALL upload a transcript within their workspace.
+- The system SHALL extract proposed action items from that transcript (PRM-001).
+- Proposals SHALL remain clearly distinct from confirmed actions and be available for review.
+
+### FR-002: Explicit review and confirmation — P0
+
+- A facilitator SHALL be able to review and set the final action text, owner, and due date.
+- The system SHALL require explicit confirmation of all three values before creating any reminder job (PRM-002).
+- The system SHALL not send, schedule, or otherwise initiate outbound delivery from an unconfirmed proposal.
+
+### FR-003: One reminder and transparent status — P0
+
+- For a confirmed action, the system SHALL deliver one reminder through the configured provider (PRM-003).
+- The system SHALL expose a delivered or failed delivery status to the facilitator.
+- Provider failure SHALL be visible and SHALL NOT trigger a silent duplicate send.
+
+### FR-004: Workspace isolation — P0
+
+- The system SHALL require workspace membership for transcript, proposal, action, and delivery-status access.
+- A user in one workspace SHALL NOT be able to read another workspace’s transcripts or actions (PRM-004).
+
+### Out of scope
+
+- Recording meetings.
+- Calendar replacement or calendar-sync workflows.
+- Autonomous outbound messaging, including delivery from unconfirmed proposals.
+- More than the single reminder required for the pilot.
+
+## Platform and integration requirements
+
+- **Platform:** responsive browser UI and Python API.
+- **Reminder integration:** exactly one configurable provider; provider selection is an E002 implementation decision.
+- **Storage:** persist transcript content, action workflow state, and delivery attempts with workspace scope.
+
+## Non-functional requirements
+
+| Area | Requirement |
+|---|---|
+| Security | Server-side workspace authorization; encryption in transit and platform encryption at rest |
+| Privacy | No regulated health or payment data in pilot scope; restrict transcript access to workspace members |
+| Reliability | Persist delivery attempts; prevent duplicate sends from retries or status polling |
+| Usability | Facilitator can understand proposal versus confirmed state and see delivery outcome without ambiguity |
+| Accessibility | Browser UI meets practical keyboard and readable-status needs; detailed WCAG target to be set during UI specification |
+| Observability | Log confirmation transition and provider outcome; error tracking is established in E000 |
+
+## Design principles
+
+1. **Human judgment is visible:** proposal, confirmation, and delivery are separate states, never hidden automation.
+2. **Trust is scoped:** every workspace view shows only its own records, enforced by the request path rather than UI filtering.
+3. **Small pilot, real proof:** use one provider and one reminder, but produce runtime evidence for the complete promise.
+
+## Constraints and reversible assumptions
+
+- Two engineers have four weeks for the pilot.
+- The chosen stack must retain a Python API and browser UI.
+- The reminder provider is not selected; an adapter keeps that choice replaceable.
+- No external market claims, pricing model, or hard capacity target are asserted because none are supplied in this workspace.
+- The plan uses two product epics plus E000 to protect the four-week boundary.
+
+## Release strategy
+
+### MVP
+
+The MVP is E000 plus E001 and E002: a facilitator can upload a transcript, review and explicitly confirm actions, send one provider-backed reminder, and see delivery status in an isolated workspace.
+
+### Delivery sequence
+
+| Phase | Epics | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| Foundation | E000 | Test, CI, environment, and observability baseline |
+| Core review | E001 | Protected transcript-to-reviewed-action workflow |
+| Delivery | E002 | One confirmed reminder with visible status |
+
+### Rollout
+
+Start with the five pilot teams after end-to-end runtime evidence and facilitator LARP pass. Keep the provider configuration reversible and rollback application releases to the preceding tested version. Do not expand to calendar features or autonomous messaging during the pilot.
+
+## Measurement plan
+
+| Metric | Target | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Proposal review rate | ≥80% | Confirmed/reviewed proposal events during pilot |
+| Unowned overdue actions | 30% reduction in four weeks | Team baseline compared with pilot-period action records |
+| Delivery transparency | Every attempted reminder has delivered or failed status | Persisted delivery-attempt records |
+| Isolation | No cross-workspace read succeeds | Required negative runtime probe using real request role |
+
+## Risks and mitigations
+
+| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Provider selection delays delivery work | Medium | High | Isolate provider behind adapter; decide in E002 before integration build |
+| Proposal quality disappoints facilitators | Medium | Medium | Keep every proposal editable and measure review rate |
+| Tenant leakage | Low | High | Server-side scoping plus real-role negative probe |
+| Scope creep into calendar or automation | Medium | High | Keep explicit non-goals and confirmation gate in every epic |
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- E001 depends on E000’s baseline test, CI, environment, and observability setup.
+- E002 depends on E001’s confirmed-action boundary and on selection/configuration of one reminder provider.
+- Runtime validation later must follow the evidence contract; no readiness claim is made by this planning artifact.
