+# Pulseboard architecture
+
+## Information sources
+
+- `project.md`: reviewed-action-item pilot, with no autonomous sending or scheduling.
+- `product-contract.md`: transcript extraction, explicit confirmation, one reminder, and workspace isolation.
+- `context.md`: two engineers, four-week pilot, Python API, browser UI, and an unselected reminder provider.
+- `evidence-contract.md`: end-to-end runtime proof, a real-role isolation probe, and facilitator LARP.
+
+No research reports or brownfield artifacts exist in this workspace. The specific framework, hosting platform, and reminder provider remain reversible implementation decisions rather than product claims.
+
+## Executive summary
+
+Pulseboard will be a small modular monolith: a browser UI talks to one Python API, which stores workspace-scoped records and invokes a pluggable reminder-provider adapter only after facilitator confirmation. This is deliberately sized for a four-week, two-engineer pilot while preserving a clear seam around external delivery.
+
+## Architectural style
+
+- **Primary style:** modular monolith with a synchronous HTTP API and a background delivery worker.
+- **Modules:** workspace access, transcript intake and extraction, action review, reminder delivery, and audit/status.
+- **Trade-off:** one deployable unit reduces pilot complexity; a provider adapter and durable delivery jobs prevent the reminder vendor from becoming a core-domain dependency.
+
+## System architecture
+
+```text
+Facilitator browser
+        |
+        v
+Python API ──> relational data store
+   |   |              |- workspaces + memberships
+   |   |              |- transcripts + proposed actions
+   |   |              |- confirmed actions + delivery attempts
+   |   v
+   |  transcript/object storage
+   v
+background delivery worker ──> configurable reminder provider
+```
+
+### Component responsibilities
+
+| Component | Responsibility | Boundary |
+|---|---|---|
+| Browser UI | Upload transcript; review and confirm actions; view delivery status | Never sends a reminder directly |
+| Python API | Authenticates caller, enforces workspace scope, validates confirmation, creates delivery jobs | Owns all user-facing workflow rules |
+| Extraction module | Turns uploaded transcript content into proposed actions | Produces proposals only; cannot schedule |
+| Relational store | Persists tenant-scoped domain records and delivery attempts | Every query is scoped by workspace |
+| Delivery worker + adapter | Sends one confirmed reminder and records its outcome | Receives confirmed jobs only; provider is swappable |
+
+### Core data flow
+
+1. A facilitator uploads a transcript within an authenticated workspace.
+2. The API records the transcript and creates proposed action items; each proposal is reviewable and editable.
+3. The facilitator explicitly confirms action text, owner, and due date. Only this transition creates a delivery job.
+4. The worker asks the configured provider to send one reminder, records delivered or failed status, and exposes that status to the workspace.
+
+## Technology strategy
+
+| Layer | Decision for the pilot | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Browser UI | Browser-based responsive UI; framework remains an E000 implementation choice | The supplied foundation requires a browser UI, not a native client |
+| API | Python HTTP API | Required by context |
+| Domain logic | Isolated Python modules for review and delivery rules | Keeps the human-confirmation rule testable |
+| Data | Relational, workspace-keyed persistence plus transcript/object storage | Supports ownership, due dates, delivery attempts, and tenant isolation |
+| Delivery | Provider adapter behind a durable job record | Reminder provider is intentionally unselected |
+| Observability | Structured audit events and error reporting | Required to prove confirmation and delivery behavior |
+
+## Cross-cutting concerns
+
+### Security and tenancy
+
+- Authenticate every browser request and derive the active workspace server-side.
+- Authorize transcript, proposal, action, and delivery-status access by workspace membership.
+- Treat a cross-workspace read attempt using the real request role as a required negative test.
+- Encrypt data in transit; use platform encryption at rest. No regulated health or payment data is in scope.
+
+### Reliability and performance
+
+- Make reminder delivery idempotent by tying each provider call to one confirmed delivery job.
+- Record provider failure separately from confirmation; do not silently retry into duplicate reminders.
+- Pilot target: normal review and status reads feel immediate; delivery can complete asynchronously with visible status.
+- Back up relational data and transcript storage according to the selected hosting platform before pilot use.
+
+## Data architecture
+
+```text
+Workspace --< Membership
+Workspace --< Transcript --< ProposedAction
+Workspace --< ConfirmedAction --< DeliveryAttempt
+```
+
+Each domain record carries a workspace identity. A proposed action is not a confirmed action: confirmation captures final text, owner, and due date as a deliberate state transition.
+
+## Integration architecture
+
+| Integration | Purpose | Contract | Fallback |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Reminder provider (TBD) | Deliver the single confirmed reminder | Adapter accepts a confirmed delivery job and returns a provider result | Keep the job pending/failed with visible status; do not send through another provider automatically |
+| Transcript extraction capability | Produce proposed actions | Receives stored transcript content and returns proposals | Show a recoverable extraction failure; facilitator may retry or enter actions later if that is added to scope |
+
+The API is versioned internally for the pilot; implementation will publish an API contract before any external integration is introduced.
+
+## Development and deployment
+
+- One repository and one deployable application with a separately runnable worker process.
+- E000 establishes formatter, tests, CI, environment configuration, and error tracking before feature implementation.
+- Use development, staging, and pilot environments; deploy only a tested build and retain a rollback to the prior application version.
+- Migrations must be backward-compatible during the pilot and validated before deployment.
+
+## Architecture decisions
+
+### ADR-001: Human confirmation is the scheduling boundary
+
+- **Status:** Accepted
+- **Decision:** Proposal extraction may create only reviewable proposed actions. A reminder job can be created only after a facilitator confirms text, owner, and due date.
+- **Consequence:** This directly preserves PRM-002 and makes the prohibited autonomous path testable.
+
+### ADR-002: Workspace isolation is enforced in the API and data access layer
+
+- **Status:** Accepted
+- **Decision:** Workspace membership is required for all resource access; all reads and writes are scoped by the server-derived workspace identity.
+- **Consequence:** The data model and request path can support the required real-role negative probe for PRM-004.
+
+### ADR-003: Reminder delivery is provider-agnostic
+
+- **Status:** Accepted
+- **Decision:** The core application owns delivery jobs and status; a selected provider is accessed only through an adapter.
+- **Consequence:** Provider selection remains reversible without changing the review workflow or delivery audit trail.
+
+## Risks and evolution
+
+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
+|---|---|---|
+| Reminder provider is not selected | Delivery cannot be validated until a provider is configured | Keep the adapter boundary explicit; choose and configure one provider in the delivery epic |
+| Extraction quality produces weak proposals | Facilitators spend too long correcting actions | Measure proposal-review rate during the pilot and keep review editable |
+| Tenant scoping is missed in a new query | Confidentiality breach | Centralize access checks and require the negative isolation probe |
+| Four-week schedule | Pilot scope slips | Keep the plan to E000 plus two product epics; defer calendar and outbound automation |
+
+Revisit the architecture after the pilot if five teams demonstrate sustained use or provider throughput requires separating the worker. Until then, the modular monolith is the lowest-complexity fit for the supplied constraints.
